faq:
  - q: "What is Meta's hiring process?"
    a: "Meta's hiring process for most technical and corporate roles: (1) Resume screen and recruiter reach-out. (2) Recruiter phone screen — background, compensation, basic fit. (3) Technical screen or role-specific screen. (4) Virtual onsite loop — typically 4–6 rounds covering technical skills (coding, system design for SWE), product sense, analytical thinking, and behavioral. (5) Hiring committee review. (6) Team matching for approved hires. Meta moves relatively quickly — most loops complete in 3–6 weeks. The company rehires layoff alumni readily and has active returnship pathways."
  - q: "What does Meta look for in candidates?"
    a: "Meta evaluates candidates against its core values — Move Fast, Be Direct, Be Open, Build Social Value — and four functional competencies: (1) Problem Solving — structured, first-principles thinking applied to real challenges. (2) Communication — clear, direct, precise communication under pressure. (3) Execution — delivering results with urgency and ownership. (4) Collaboration — working across functions, especially in complex multi-stakeholder environments. Unlike Amazon, Meta does not use a published LP framework — but interviewers assess all of these consistently."
  - q: "How are Meta's coding interviews different from other FAANG companies?"
    a: "Meta's coding interviews tend to be slightly more focused on efficiency and optimization than Microsoft's — but less rigidly scored than Google's. Meta interviewers often let candidates talk through their approach first, then code. The most important difference: Meta frequently asks questions about graphs, trees, and dynamic programming at higher frequency than some other top companies. Meta also has a reputation for asking real-world-inspired problems (how would you process a social graph?) that test both coding and system design thinking simultaneously."
  - q: "How competitive is it to get a job at Meta?"
    a: "Very competitive for engineering and product roles. Meta is one of the highest-paying tech companies in the world, which makes competition intense. However, Meta has also gone through significant hiring cycles — expanding rapidly in 2020–2021, laying off 11,000+ in 2022, and 10,000+ in 2023. Post-layoff, the bar for rehires and new hires has been recalibrated. Meta actively recruits from its own layoff alumni through a dedicated returnship program."
  - q: "What is team matching at Meta?"
    a: "After a hiring committee approves your hire, Meta assigns you to a team through a matching process. Unlike Google, where matching can take weeks, Meta's process is typically faster — you may interview with specific teams during the onsite loop, or be matched post-approval. You can express team preferences and will typically have conversations with 2–3 teams before being placed. Having a specific team in mind — and doing research on it — accelerates the matching process."
